  "bloques": [
    {
      "tipo": "explicacion",
      "titulo": {
        "en": "The moves of a conversation"
      },
      "texto": {
        "en": "**Opening a story.** **¿Sabes qué ha pasado?** **Oye, tengo que contarte una cosa.** The other person can answer **No, ¿qué ha pasado?**, **Dime** or, if they know, **Sí, ya lo sé** / **Ya me lo han contado**.\n\n**Showing you are following.** Use reactions such as **Sí, sí, claro**, **Ya**, **¿De verdad?**, **¿En serio?**, **¡Vaya!**, **¡Qué bien!** or **¡Qué horror!** Their force depends on context and intonation.\n\n**Moving the story on.** **Y entonces…** introduces the next event; **de repente** signals a sudden event; **al final** introduces the eventual outcome.\n\n**Checking.** **¿Me entiendes?** asks whether someone is following. **¿No?** and **¿eh?** are informal and depend on tone. On the phone: **¿Me oyes? Te oigo fatal. Se ha cortado.**\n\n**Managing interruptions.** To enter politely: **Perdona, ¿puedo decir una cosa?** To give the turn back: **Sigue, sigue** / **Continúa, por favor.** To ask to keep the floor: **Déjame terminar, por favor** / **Solo un minuto**; these can sound firm depending on tone.\n\n**Changing the topic.** **Por cierto…** and **a propósito…** introduce a side remark. **Otra cosa…** brings up a new point; **en cuanto a…** frames what you are about to comment on.\n\n**Adding a focus.** **Sobre todo** means especially; **en concreto** and **en particular** specify something.\n\n**Wrapping up.** **En resumen** and **para terminar** mark a summary or final point. **Al final** reports the outcome of events; it does not by itself close a conversation. **Bueno, pues nada…** can signal an informal closing, often followed by **Vale, nos vemos** or **Venga, hasta luego**."
      }
    },
